{
  "title": "UML3 to database schemas: SQL DDL and NoSQL",
  "description": "Can a UML3 model be turned into a schema, and read back from one, across the engines most projects actually use? Each row is something a database expresses, how the major engines spell it, and which UML3 construct carries it. The aim is breadth of vendor coverage without breadth of language: almost every row below is a library element or a marker on an existing one, per docs/UML3-LAYERING.md, so covering another engine costs a library and not a new construct. UML3Data already models the logical and physical sides and the #mapsTo link between them; the interesting rows are the physical details a CREATE statement needs, and the ones where a document, wide-column or graph store has no relational equivalent at all.",
  "coverageLegend": {
    "COVERED": "a UML3 construct expresses it; the construct is named in the UML3 column",
    "PARTIAL": "expressible, but with loss - the note says what is lost",
    "GAP": "no UML3 construct expresses it; a requirement is needed if it matters",
    "TBD": "not yet analyzed, or the dialect's behaviour has not been confirmed"
  },
  "versionBasis": "Relational columns are written against PostgreSQL 17, MySQL 8.4, Microsoft SQL Server 2022 and Oracle 23ai, and against ISO/IEC 9075:2023 where the standard settles it. Non-relational columns are written against MongoDB 8, Apache Cassandra 5, Amazon DynamoDB and Neo4j 5. No database engine is installed on this machine, so unlike the Java and Rust columns of the language map nothing here has been executed; rows whose answer depends on engine behaviour are marked TBD. NA in a target column means that target has no counterpart for the concept, which is a decision and not an omission; where the answer is not yet known the coverage column says TBD instead.",
